---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Installer from '../components/Installer.astro';
---
<Layout title="A little order for your shell" description="Manage your Bash and Zsh environment across macOS and Linux. Predictable paths, optional tool modules, and a repeatable installer that respects your configuration.">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div class="hero-copy"><div class="release-pill"><span class="status-dot"></span> v0.1.1 <span> / </span> macOS + Linux</div>
      <h1>Your shell.<br />A little more <em>order.</em></h1>
      <p class="hero-description">Bring your paths, tools, and shell configuration together. Set up once, understand what runs, and feel at home on your next machine.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button" href="#install">Install EnvManager <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/docs/">Explore the docs <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      <div class="compatibility"><span>Works where you do</span><span class="os-label">◉ macOS</span><span class="os-label">⌘ Linux</span><span class="shell-label">Bash / Zsh</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-visual" aria-label="Example EnvManager shell configuration">
      <div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-title"><div class="traffic"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><span>your-shell.sh</span><span>~</span></div>
        <div class="terminal-code"><p class="comment"># A place for everything.</p><p><span class="syntax">em_add_path</span> PATH <span class="string">"$HOME/bin"</span></p><p class="comment gap"># Priority, when you need it.</p><p><span class="syntax">em_add_path</span> PATH <span class="string">"$HOME/work/bin"</span> prepend-move</p><p class="comment gap"># Your tools. Your choice.</p><p><span class="syntax">em_enable</span> nvm python</p><p class="comment gap"># Keep parallel work under control.</p><p><span class="syntax">em_run_concurrent</span> <span class="string">--key builds</span> 2 npm run build</p><p class="terminal-prompt"><span>❯</span> <b></b></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="floating-label"><span class="check-symbol">✓</span><div>Same command. Same setup.<small>Already installed? You're already home.</small></div></div>
      <span class="visual-caption">SMALL HELPERS. LESS SHELL HOUSEKEEPING.</span>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="principles wrap"><span><b>01</b> Predictable paths</span><span><b>02</b> Tools by choice</span><span><b>03</b> Portable configuration</span><span><b>04</b> Repeatable setup</span></div>
  <section class="capabilities wrap"><div class="section-intro"><span class="eyebrow">THE EVERYDAY DETAILS, HANDLED</span><h2>A calmer place<br />to get things done.</h2><p>Keep the useful parts of your setup together, with plain shell files you can read and change.</p></div>
    <div class="feature-list">
      <article><span class="feature-icon">↳</span><div><h3>Paths that stay in their lane.</h3><p>Append, prepend, or move an entry to the front. Keep existing entries in place and preserve system manual paths.</p><code>em_add_path PATH "$HOME/bin"</code></div></article>
      <article><span class="feature-icon">⊞</span><div><h3>Start small. Add what you use.</h3><p>The core preset starts with environment helpers, user binary paths, and telemetry preferences. Enable optional tools when you're ready.</p><code>em_enable nvm</code></div></article>
      <article><span class="feature-icon">⇄</span><div><h3>Share the machine, politely.</h3><p>Limit matching commands across shell sessions. Waiting work runs when a slot opens, with the original arguments and exit status.</p><code>em_run_concurrent --key builds 2 npm run build</code></div></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="setup-section" id="install"><div class="wrap setup-grid"><div class="setup-copy"><span class="eyebrow">A FAMILIAR FIRST STEP</span><h2>One command.<br />Then make it yours.</h2><p>The installer detects your shell, adds a managed startup block, and keeps your existing configuration. Run it again to repair a missing integration.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/#installation-behavior">See exactly what changes →</a></div><Installer /></div></section>
  <section class="closing wrap"><span class="eyebrow">PLAIN FILES. CLEAR INTENT.</span><h2>Your configuration belongs to you.</h2><p>Inspect the installer, keep local overrides, and upgrade when you choose. EnvManager is built to be understood.</p><a href="https://github.com/VictorQueiroz/envmanager" class="text-link">Read the source on GitHub ↗</a></section>
</Layout>
